// A glyph shader supplies two functions, compiled after the declarations above: vertex Out arkos_glyph_vertex(uint unit_vertex_id [[vertex_id]], uint sprite_id [[instance_id]], constant float2 *unit_vertices [[buffer(SpriteInputIndex_Vertices)]], constant MonochromeSprite *sprites [[buffer(SpriteInputIndex_Sprites)]], constant Size_DevicePixels *viewport_size [[buffer(SpriteInputIndex_ViewportSize)]], constant Size_DevicePixels *atlas_size [[buffer(SpriteInputIndex_AtlasTextureSize)]]) and fragment float4 arkos_glyph_fragment(In input [[stage_in]], constant MonochromeSprite *sprites [[buffer(SpriteInputIndex_Sprites)]], texture2d<float> atlas_texture [[texture(SpriteInputIndex_AtlasTexture)]]), where Out is a struct of your own with a [[position]] field and In the same fields as the fragment's stage input.
// A glyph shader may also draw the window's emoji (never its pictures, which keep gpui's stages) by supplying fragment float4 arkos_emoji_fragment(In input [[stage_in]], constant PolychromeSprite *sprites [[buffer(SpriteInputIndex_Sprites)]], texture2d<float> atlas_texture [[texture(SpriteInputIndex_AtlasTexture)]]) and, if it places them itself, vertex Out arkos_emoji_vertex(uint unit_vertex_id [[vertex_id]], uint sprite_id [[instance_id]], constant float2 *unit_vertices [[buffer(SpriteInputIndex_Vertices)]], constant PolychromeSprite *sprites [[buffer(SpriteInputIndex_Sprites)]], constant Size_DevicePixels *viewport_size [[buffer(SpriteInputIndex_ViewportSize)]], constant Size_DevicePixels *atlas_size [[buffer(SpriteInputIndex_AtlasTextureSize)]]); without that vertex function gpui's polychrome_sprite_vertex places them and In is PolychromeSpriteFragmentInput.
// Any of these functions may also take the window's effect clock, its seconds as one float at buffer 5 (SpriteInputIndex_GlyphTime), by adding the parameter constant float *time [[buffer(5)]] and reading *time; gpui's own stages ignore it.
